  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>In the heart of old Saint-Tropez, between the Portalet tower and the Tour Vieille, La Ponche and La Fontanette are two adjoining beaches of coarse sand, at the foot of the fishermen's houses of the town's oldest district (tourist office). La Ponche (from the Provençal pouncho, "point") was formerly the fishermen's old port.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>As on all the town's beaches, municipal order no. 1828/2026 of 6 July 2026 allows dogs on the town's beaches from 6am to 9.30am and 6.30pm to 10pm, from 16 July to 15 September 2026; outside those hours they are not allowed. Dogs must stay under their owner's constant responsibility and be kept on a leash when approaching other users and whenever circumstances require; the order does not apply to walkers with dogs on a leash on the coastal path. It only covers that period: for the rest of the year, we found no published rule, so rely on the signage on site. Saint-Tropez Tourisme's listing for these two beaches, however, says "dogs are not allowed" with no mention of hours — probably a listing not updated since the order, but that isn't guaranteed. Check the signage on site before settling in with your dog.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: in summer, the allowed windows for your dog are 6am-9.30am and 6.30pm-10pm; check the signage before settling in.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>A little further on, <a href="/en/fiche-plage-graniers-saint-tropez">Graniers beach</a> lies at the foot of the Citadel, past the marine cemetery.</p>
